{"name":"Sail to the Sea","hex":"#99c3f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of fabric attached to a boat and arranged such that it causes the wind to drive the boat along. The sail may be attached to the boat via a combination of mast, spars and ropes.","The concept of a sail or sails, as if a substance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be impelled or driven forward by the action of wind upon sails, as a ship on water; to be impelled on a body of water by steam or other power.","To move through or on the water; to swim, as a fish or a waterfowl."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sail"},{"term":"sea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Semai."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of salt water.","The ocean; the continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea"}],"entries":[{"title":"To the Sea","blocks":[{"text":"To the Sea is the fifth studio album by American singer-songwriter Jack Johnson, released on May 26, 2010, in Japan, May 31, 2010 in the United Kingdom and June 1, 2010, in the United States, through Universal Republic Records and Brushfire Music Group. The album debuted at number 1 in the United States, selling 243,000 copies in its first week."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9c/Tothesea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/To_the_Sea","wikidata":"Q1855524"},{"title":"Sail","blocks":[{"text":"A sail is a tensile structure, which is made from fabric or other membrane materials, that uses wind power to propel sailing craft, including sailing ships, sailboats, windsurfers, ice boats, and even sail-powered land vehicles. Sails may be made from a combination of woven materials—including canvas or polyester cloth, laminated membranes or bonded filaments, usually in a three- or four-sided shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Chesapeake.jpg/330px-Chesapeake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sail","wikidata":"Q25999"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg/500px-Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg/500px-Sailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASailing_Vessel_Odyssey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg/250px-L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg/250px-L-2013-Oracle-Team-USA-17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","